@meta
id: 27-09
title: Drill — keep it in this buffer
chapter: 27
type: drill
skills: set-local, buffer-switch
par: 45
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: nothing
open: report.sql total.ts

@brief
Eight times: give the SQL buffer a four-space indent without leaking it,
indent its second line, switch to the TypeScript buffer, indent its second
line by two, write both.

The TypeScript file is the check. If it comes out with four spaces you
typed `:set` when you meant `:setlocal`.

@keys
| `:setlocal shiftwidth=4` | this buffer only |
| `:bn` | next buffer |
| `:wqa` | write and quit everything |

@hints
The word is `setlocal`, not `set`.
--
`:setlocal shiftwidth=4` `Enter`, `2G>>`, `:bn` `Enter`, `2G>>`, `:wqa`.

@solution
    :setlocal shiftwidth=4<Enter>  2G>>  :bn<Enter>  2G>>  :wqa<Enter>

Forty keystrokes.

Eight reps of this is eight chances to type `:set` out of habit and watch
the second file come out wrong. That is the whole value of the drill: the
mistake is invisible in the buffer you are looking at and obvious in the
one you are not.

@start report.sql
SELECT status, count(*) FROM orders
GROUP BY status;

@expected report.sql
SELECT status, count(*) FROM orders
    GROUP BY status;

@start total.ts
export const totals = {
cancelled: 0,
};

@expected total.ts
export const totals = {
  cancelled: 0,
};
